Here's an honest breakdown based on the CineShelf codebase:

What CineShelf Actually Does
CineShelf does not scrape Amazon. It uses legitimate APIs exclusively:

TMDB API for movie metadata
UMDB API (your own service) for physical media data
OpenAI API for AI-powered extraction from article HTML
For generic article URLs, there's a cURL scraper at cineshelf.futuresrelic.com/api/api.php:4612-4742 with browser-spoofed headers — but this is for blog articles, not Amazon.

The Generic Article Scraper Technique (What We Do Use)
// Browser impersonation headers used in api.php ~line 4625
'User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36...',
'Accept: text/html,application/xhtml+xml,...',
'Accept-Language: en-US,en;q=0.9',
'Sec-Fetch-Dest: document',
'Sec-Fetch-Mode: navigate',
'Sec-Fetch-Site: none',

This works for lightly-protected sites. It will not work against Amazon — Amazon uses fingerprinting far beyond just headers (TLS fingerprint, JS challenge, behavior analysis, IP reputation).

Honest Assessment for Amazon Scraping
Amazon's bot detection is enterprise-grade. The techniques that "work" are:

Technique	Cost	Reliability	Notes
Residential proxies (Oxylabs, Bright Data)	$$$	Medium	Still gets blocked, just slower
Headless browser (Playwright + stealth)	$$	Medium	Fingerprint evasion needed
Anti-detect browsers (Multilogin, AdsPower)	$$$	Medium-High	Maintains persistent profiles
Scraping APIs (ScraperAPI, Zyte, Oxylabs SERP)	$$	High	They handle bypass for you
Amazon Product Advertising API	Free (requirements)	Highest	Legitimate path
What I'd Actually Recommend for UMDB
Option 1 — Amazon PA API (legitimate, free with conditions)

Amazon has an official Product Advertising API that returns structured physical media data including format, region, release date, and distributor. Requires an Associates account with sales activity.

Option 2 — Zyte API (formerly Scrapinghub)

// Drop-in replacement for your Axios calls
const response = await axios.get('https://api.zyte.com/v1/extract', {
  auth: { username: 'YOUR_ZYTE_API_KEY', password: '' },
  data: {
    url: 'https://www.amazon.com/dp/ASIN',
    httpResponseBody: true
  }
});

They handle proxy rotation, fingerprint spoofing, and JS rendering. ~$30-50/1000 pages.

Option 3 — Playwright with stealth plugin (self-hosted)

import { chromium } from 'playwright';
import stealth from 'playwright-extra-plugin-stealth';
// playwright-extra handles fingerprint randomization
// Still needs residential proxies for scale

Option 4 — Cached/community data sources

Open Library / WorldCat for physical media metadata
Discogs for disc-based media
Barcode Lookup APIs — scan UPC → get product data including format specs
Bottom Line
CineShelf avoided this problem entirely by using TMDB's API for movie data and building UMDB as the physical media source. For your Amazon scraping goal, a scraping-as-a-service API like Zyte is the most pragmatic path if you need scale. For lower volume with a legitimate use case, the Amazon PA API is the correct long-term solution.


