Short answer: No, and you likely shouldn't.

Here's the breakdown:

No Official API
Blu-ray.com does not offer a publicly documented API. There's no sanctioned way to programmatically fetch covers or metadata from them.

Copyright on Images
Cover art (DVD/Blu-ray) is copyrighted by the studios, not blu-ray.com. Even if you scraped the images, you'd need a license from each studio — not from the site.

Scraping Risk
The site returns 403 errors to automated requests, which signals they actively block scraping. Their ToS almost certainly prohibits it (standard for commercial content sites).

Better Alternatives for Cover Art

Source	Notes
The Movie Database (TMDB)	Free API, community-maintained, extensive cover art
Open Movie Database (OMDB)	Free tier available, pulls from IMDb
Fanart.tv	High-quality fan-made art, free API
MusicBrainz/Cover Art Archive	Open source, free
TMDB is the standard choice for projects like yours (CineShelf). It's free, has generous rate limits, and explicitly allows use in apps. Most media apps (Plex, Jellyfin, Kodi) use it.

